## Context

The score reads declared dimensions — importance, durability, whether a memory
prevents an action or answers a question, the cost of rediscovering it, its
scope. New memories will declare them at writing. **The roughly 950 that already
exist declare nothing**, so on the day the scorer ships it would rank a corpus
of blanks, and every ticket downstream would be exercised against a degenerate
input.

So the existing corpus needs a one-off seeding pass, and judging what a memory
is worth is not something a predicate does. A model reads each body and
proposes its dimensions.

Design reference: P4's importance axis and P5 in `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design.md`.

## Relevant files

- `projects/*/memory/*.md` and `memory/*.md` — the corpus to annotate
- `skills/memory/SKILL.md` — where the same dimensions are declared going forward

## Actions

1. Fix the dimension vocabulary first, in the skill, so the pass annotates
   against a written definition rather than inventing one per body.
2. Run the model over every live body. It reads the body and proposes values.
3. Land the annotations as a data change, reviewable by count and spot-check
   like `0911` — not by reading 950 diffs. State the distribution: how many
   guards, how many references, the spread of importance.
4. Record the model's identity and the prompt, so a later reader knows what
   produced the numbers and can re-run with a better prompt.

## Test

Red first: a body with no dimensions is rejected by the scorer's input check,
so the gap is visible before the pass rather than after. Then a hand-scored
sample of a dozen bodies as a calibration set — if the pass disagrees with the
sample on the guard/reference distinction, the vocabulary is wrong, not the
bodies.

## Verification

- [ ] Every live body carries every dimension, or is listed as skipped with a reason
- [ ] The calibration sample agrees on the guard/reference split
- [ ] Distribution reported in the pull request

## Invariants

- **The model proposes dimensions; it does not rank.** Writing a field is
  authorship, which is the model's side of the line; combining fields into an
  order is the scorer's, and stays deterministic. The boundary in §5 is not
  crossed by this ticket and must not be.
- Nothing is deleted, and the pass is re-runnable: a better prompt can replace
  the annotations, which is why getting it imperfect is survivable.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The dimension vocabulary is written down before the pass runs
- [ ] Every live body annotated, coverage stated as a count
- [ ] Calibration sample passes
- [ ] Model identity and prompt recorded
